th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028+v3-2026-11-02+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+type-mix-retire-20261112+type-mix-topup-20261112+type-mix-retire2-20261112+fill-20261112+fill2-20261112","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Programming-for-EOR/comparison-operators.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Programming-for-EOR/comparison-operators.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a numeric vector `pay` with 8 elements and no missing values, what does the expression `pay > 3000` return?","options":["The elements of `pay` that are larger than 3000, in their original order","A single `TRUE` if at least one element of `pay` is larger than 3000","Eight `TRUE`/`FALSE` values, one per element of `pay`, in the same order","The positions of the elements of `pay` that are larger than 3000"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2dd256eb883f18e354f5a74e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"After `a <- 0.7 - 0.4`, a student wants to check whether `a` equals 0.3. Which expression gives a reliable answer?","options":["`a == 0.3`","`abs(a - 0.3) < 1e-9`","`a - 0.3 < 1e-9`","`a = 0.3`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66b28e09dbbb8748c3a75aeb","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which R expression asks, for each element of a numeric vector `x`, whether that element is different from 4?","options":["`x <> 4`","`x =! 4`","`x ~= 4`","`x != 4`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e20890f3876ab252ce21cd96","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(7, 2, 4, 9) >= 4`?","options":["`[1]  TRUE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE  TRUE`","`[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE FALSE`","`[1] 7 4 9`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1182bc3174ebcb9aae3d916d","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(3, 5, 1) > c(2, 6, 1)`?","options":["`[1]  TRUE FALSE  TRUE`","`[1] FALSE`","`[1] FALSE  TRUE FALSE`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e83c291f3d4a6330ed7e5fa4","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A column of city names was typed by hand as `city <- c(\"Groningen\", \"groningen\", \" Groningen\", \"Assen\")`. What does `city == \"Groningen\"` return?","options":["`[1]  TRUE  TRUE FALSE FALSE`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE  TRUE FALSE`","`[1]  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE FALSE`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"44bd2693c3cbfcf62a97a593","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A student runs `x <- c(5, 8, 2); x = 8; x`, intending the middle statement to ask which elements equal 8. What does the final `x` print?","options":["`[1] FALSE  TRUE FALSE`","`[1] 5 8 2`","`[1] 8`","`[1] 8 8 8`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c482cb00afc04155ad210c08","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(4, NA, 1) < 3`?","options":["`[1] FALSE    NA  TRUE`","`[1] FALSE FALSE  TRUE`","`[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE`","`[1] NA`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"abda3db97a70e2b0f0c67435","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(TRUE, FALSE, TRUE) == 1`?","options":["`[1] FALSE FALSE FALSE`","`[1] 1 0 1`","the error `comparison (==) is possible only for atomic and list types`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE  TRUE`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3963c94663dd79de6d779332","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For `x <- c(2, 4, 6)`, which comparison prints `[1] FALSE  TRUE FALSE`?","options":["`x != 4`","`x == 4`","`x >= 4`","`x < 4`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cdc1375356bd6e9e63900053","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a numeric vector `x` without missing values, which comparison gives the opposite answer of `x > 4` in every position?","options":["`x < 4`","`x >= 4`","`x <= 4`","`x != 4`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e5caecda2866c8fcdbaf1b78","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"After `x <- c(-6, 2, 9)`, a student types `x<-4` without spaces, meaning to ask which elements are below minus 4. What happens?","options":["`x` becomes the single number `4`; the vector is gone.","`x` is unchanged and R prints `[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE`.","`x` is unchanged and R stops with a syntax error.","`x` becomes the single number `-4`; the vector is gone."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8a5ebefa2b6a597633f2695f","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(2, 3, 4) * 2 >= 6`?","options":["`[1] 0 0 0`","`[1] 6 8`","`[1] FALSE FALSE  TRUE`","`[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c6d73315a03096f6d2545b3c","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For `x <- c(NA, 2, 5)`, what does R print for `x == x`?","options":["`[1] TRUE TRUE TRUE`","`[1]   NA TRUE TRUE`","`[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE`","`[1] NA NA NA`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"282404efaa379fc2ec792bee","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"To mark the elements of `x <- c(1, 4, 9)` that satisfy $2<x_i<6$, a student types `2 < x < 6` at the console. What happens?","options":["R returns `[1] FALSE  TRUE FALSE`, as the mathematical notation suggests.","R returns `[1] TRUE TRUE TRUE`, after comparing the answers of `2 < x` with 6.","R stops with `Error: unexpected '<' in \"2 < x <\"`.","R returns `[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE`, using only the part `2 < x`."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"892f2dc35bcbbbb08602ecea","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What does R print for `c(\"pear\", \"grain\", \"fig\", \"apricot\") < \"grape\"`?","options":["`[1] FALSE  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE  TRUE FALSE`","`[1] FALSE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE`","`[1] NA NA NA NA`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"871c63e1e461fef79cfa8330","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In R, `0.5 + 0.25 == 0.75` returns `TRUE`, while `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` returns `FALSE`. Which statement explains the difference?","options":["`==` compares to about 15 significant digits, and the error in `0.1 + 0.2` is larger than that.","Numbers below 0.5 lose precision when they are stored, while larger numbers are stored exactly.","`==` compares the printed values, and `0.1 + 0.2` is printed with more digits than `0.3`.","0.5, 0.25 and 0.75 have exact binary representations, while 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 do not."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"12a4f94df4da7f13fce9d7d6","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"The call `x <- seq(0, 1, by = 0.1)` creates the 11 values 0, 0.1, 0.2, …, 1, and R prints the fourth of them as `0.3`. What does `sum(x == 0.3)` return?","options":["`[1] 4`","`[1] 0`","`[1] 1`","`[1] 11`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba99109161dc2cda806764fd","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A data file stored a quantity as text, `q <- c(\"5\", \"05\", \"5.0\")`. What does R return for `q == 5`?","options":["`[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE`","`[1] TRUE TRUE TRUE`","`[1] FALSE FALSE FALSE`","`[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE`, with the warning `NAs introduced by coercion`"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d6e21b2a9d51841d7a2bfae","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_programming_for_eor_comparison_operators_a2a27a2d_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For `y <- c(2, 6, 9)`, a student wants the elements equal to 6 and types `y[y = 6]`. What is the result?","options":["`[1] 6`, and `y` is unchanged","`[1] 6`, and `y` is now the single number 6","`[1] NA`, and `y` is unchanged","a syntax error, because `=` is not allowed inside square brackets"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c8e28b774167ce0f86db0264","time_limit":40}]}}